// `regex.try_compile` — the recoverable twin of `regex.compile`, on the `json.parse`/`json.try_parse`
// model. `regex_invalid_pattern.noe` pins the aborting half: a bad pattern there stops the program,
// which is the right report for a pattern written as program text. This is the other caller — a
// pattern read from configuration or handed over by a user, where the caller cannot validate it
// first (that would mean shipping a second, worse regex engine) and cannot report the failure at
// all through a door that aborts.
//
// The `Err` side is a plain `string` carrying the engine's own caret-and-span diagnostic — the whole
// value, since a pattern has exactly one way to be invalid. The two doors cannot drift: the aborting
// one is derived from this one, and prefixes its own name onto the same text.
// expect: stdout "ok \\d+"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "err carries the engine diagnostic"
// expect: stdout "still running"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "false"
// expect: exit 0
use std.{regex}
use std.regex.Pattern

// A valid pattern comes back through the recoverable door indistinguishable from the aborting one.
echo match regex.try_compile('\d+') {
    Ok(p) => "ok ${p.source()}",
    Err(e) => "unreached ${e}",
}

good = match regex.try_compile('\d+') {
    Ok(p) => p,
    Err(e) => panic("expected a valid pattern"),
}
echo good is Pattern

// A malformed one is an ordinary value, not an abort — and the message is the engine's, not a
// paraphrase of it.
echo match regex.try_compile('a(b') {
    Ok(p) => "unreached",
    Err(e) => match e.index_of("unclosed group") {
        some(i) => "err carries the engine diagnostic",
        none => "unexpected message: ${e}",
    },
}

// The program is still here, which is the whole point.
echo "still running"

// Lookaround and backreferences are compile failures of this engine, so they reach the recoverable
// door as ordinary `Err`s too, rather than a separate category.
echo match regex.try_compile('(?=foo)') {
    Ok(p) => false,
    Err(e) => true,
}

// A pattern from either door matches identically.
echo good.is_match("a1b");
echo good.is_match("abc");
